Gradle started with a dynamic Groovy DSL, then introduced a static Kotlin DSL, and is now moving towards a purely declarative configuration language inspired by Kotlin. We’ll discuss the evolution of Gradle DSLs and highlight the lessons learned and the tangible benefits enabled by each step of the journey.

In this talk, we will discuss the trade-offs between flexibility and other factors such as maintainability, user experience, and IDE support. We’ll explore the distinction between software definition and build logic and how separating them satisfies the distinct needs of software developers, build engineers, and plugin developers.

Finally, we will also discuss how to make the most of the Kotlin DSL in the present, while preparing for the future.','{"id": "ad8dfdce557f41b9bf379cfbcb8f15c5", "room": "Room 13a", "title": "A tale of the Gradle DSLs", "endsAt": 1779354000000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Extensibility/Tooling]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Paul Merlin]", "startsAt": 1779351300000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-21", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T11:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T10:15:00"}'),
