4193,-0.025985718,-0.021347046,0.018661499,-0.006713867,-0.0070762634,0.022155762,-0.00774765,-0.029006958,0.011558533,0.030822754,0.01701355,0.013061523,-0.015281677,-0.013511658,0.010902405,-0.0128479,-0.02154541,-0.01713562,0.0056610107,0.002855301,0.04031372,0.0077705383,0.025390625,-0.039916992,0.0087890625,0.019195557,-0.01259613,-0.069885254,0.022750854,-0.014060974,0.0045051575,0.012199402,0.0013418198,-0.025436401,0.046203613,-0.003314972,0.005001068,-0.00047898293,-0.0030174255,0.021621704,0.0038490295,0.00012564659,-0.010154724,0.017684937,0.0021629333,0.031951904,0.016845703,0.02633667,0.01663208,0.0007247925,-0.030853271,0.027130127,0.005191803,0.034088135,-0.007320404,-0.01574707,-0.0047798157,-0.003194809,0.017547607,-0.0050697327,0.035247803,0.015296936,0.0059890747,-0.022216797,0.016113281,-0.0102005005,0.00793457,-0.008224487,0.011528015,0.008575439,0.026168823,0.035003662,-0.03338623,0.0107803345,-0.00024139881,-0.016235352,-0.016220093,-0.008811951,0.013427734,-0.009048462,0.013633728,0.014167786,-0.027786255,0.023162842,0.0017175674,0.0075683594,-0.014289856,-0.0020275116,0.016036987,-0.029296875,-0.0030536652,0.0059432983,-0.03237915,0.0034046173,-0.022659302,0.03665161,-0.0040740967,-0.0018424988,0.013000488,-0.014678955,0.0016107559,-0.015914917,-0.011802673,-0.028213501]'::public.vector,'title:Local Lifetimes for Kotlin, description:Many objects are meant to be used only "locally", in the sense that they should not escape from the current function into the "global" world. For example, buildList is given a function that is meant to be used solely by buildList, and buildList calls that function with a MutableList that is meant to be used only by that function. Kotlin''s experimental CallsInPlace contract can enforce the former, but currently there is nothing for the latter.

In this talk, I will present a preliminary design we have developed for adding enforceable "locality" to Kotlin as a lightweight form of limited-lifetime objects. With it, library designers can let users write blocks that are guaranteed to have only temporary access to privileged objects. Those objects can be mutable views for building collections, or they can be capabilities indicating that code is executing within the critical section of some lock. Similarly, developers can use locality to prevent accidentally leaking temporary internal objects.

Beyond preventing bugs, locality enables more advanced forms of control. Because local objects cannot outlive the function call that created them, they can safely break to or return from that function, in addition to accessing the current state of its local variables. They can even suspend computation when called within a suspendable function. So locality provides a surprisingly lightweight form of (algebraic) effect polymorphism, with which one can build systems that can work in both synchronous and asynchronous settings.

Besides new functionality, I will cover how this design integrates into the existing Kotlin ecosystem. The design has an "opt-in" nature that lets libraries adopt it as the need arises. At the same time, it is highly backwards compatible, so such adoption can be guaranteed to not break existing users (except where intended to disallow misusages of the library). And while some of the design enables platform-specific optimizations, like stack-allocating objects in Kotlin/Native, overall it can integrate well with existing interopability across platforms. Finally, type-outference enables this new functionality to be checked reliably and efficiently.','{"id": "c39732e600e74f7d9ed320abb1e18c7f", "room": "Keynote, Room 14", "title": "Local Lifetimes for Kotlin", "endsAt": 1779367500000, "category": "[Regular session, Advanced, Language and best practices]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Ross Tate]", "startsAt": 1779364800000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-21", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T14:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T14:00:00"}'),
