Using built-in Web AI allows sensitive data to be processed locally, improves the responsiveness of AI-driven features, and enables offline usage. At the same time, these new APIs come with limitations that may affect performance, availability, and real-world applicability.

In this talk, we take a look at the current state of Web AI: what capabilities are already available, how these APIs work, and where they fall short. We discuss the practical advantages and trade-offs of adopting browser-native AI to help you make informed decisions.

To make this more concrete, we''ll walk through some examples demonstrating how to invoke Web AI APIs to build features such as real-time text summarization, translation, and other AI-powered functionalities. All run directly in the user''s browser.','{"id": "dec8da57fe2049208073817ae871b09b", "room": "Room 5, Lightning talks", "title": "Building AI‑Powered Web Apps in Kotlin/Wasm", "endsAt": 1779367500000, "category": "[Lightning talk, Introductory and overview, Multiplatform, AI]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Zalim Bashorov]", "startsAt": 1779366600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-21", "durationMinutes": 15, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T14:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T14:30:00"}'),
