In this live experiment, two developers will use leading AI tools (including JetBrains Junie and Claude Code) to build identical Kotlin applications from scratch: a Ktor backend, a Kotlin.js frontend, tests, and deployment. 35 minutes. No rehearsal.
We''re testing whether these AIs can actually:
Navigate Kotlin''s type system (nullable types, sealed classes, reified generics)
Write idiomatic coroutines and Flow transformations
Use Kotlin-specific libraries (kotlinx.serialization, Ktor, Exposed)
Debug compiler errors that aren''t just "semicolon missing"
Generate code that doesn''t make Kotlin developers cry
The rumor is that LLMs are trained mostly on Python/JS codebases. If that''s true, we''re about to find out—in the most entertaining way possible.
Expect spectacular failures. Bring skepticism of epic proportions and bad jokes. Let''s find out if AI can Kotlin.
You decide: After watching both attempts succeed (or struggle), vote on whether these tools are ready for production Kotlin work, or if we''re still better off with good old IntelliJ muscle memory.','{"id": "abaf500489494f57968ac2bacda8c6a4", "room": "Auditorium, Room 1", "title": "Robocoders: The [K]agematch", "endsAt": 1779379200000, "category": "[Regular session, Introductory and overview, Server-side, Multiplatform, Language and best practices, AI]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Viktor Gamov, Baruch Sadogursky]", "startsAt": 1779376500000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-21", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T18:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T17:15:00"}'),
