In this talk, I’ll share the journey of how the JVM Platform team introduced Kotlin into Uber’s backend environment: how we made the business case to engineering leads, worked side-by-side with the Java platform and feature teams, addressed developer skepticism, and tackled technical gaps in build tooling, static analysis, and monorepo tooling. This isn’t just “another language switch”, but rather enabling thousands of engineers to evolve together with modern programming paradigms. Whether you’re an engineering leader or a hands-on developer, you’ll see how a large-scale transformation can start with one language choice and ripple outward.','{"id": "714d6e45a7494227b51c59d1209a1732", "room": "Room 13a", "title": "Go Get It, with Kotlin: Evolving Uber’s Java Backend", "endsAt": 1779379200000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Server-side, Language and best practices]", "dayIndex": 1, "speakers": "[Ryan Ulep]", "startsAt": 1779376500000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-21", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T18:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-21T17:15:00"}'),
