8468,-0.023223877,-0.015975952,0.0028572083,0.005607605,0.005256653,0.02734375,0.002632141,-0.032348633,-0.0016880035,0.012084961,0.032409668,0.02078247,-0.0045547485,0.019927979,0.021087646,-0.0025196075,-0.035003662,0.0071029663,-0.010032654,0.0032291412,0.021148682,0.010826111,0.0056610107,-0.01889038,-0.0087890625,0.020507812,0.014099121,-0.047027588,-0.015899658,-0.018798828,0.01890564,0.028549194,-0.009048462,-0.021759033,0.03250122,0.02053833,-0.013450623,0.009140015,0.00048351288,0.053344727,0.010734558,-0.01259613,-0.0014734268,0.016082764,-0.009429932,0.0119018555,0.0037765503,0.016662598,-0.008735657,0.011184692,0.005680084,0.0064468384,0.002363205,0.039916992,-0.009841919,0.0059013367,-0.012702942,-0.013465881,0.016052246,-0.02355957,0.025848389,0.034820557,0.008590698,-0.023025513,0.038085938,-0.010299683,0.0016555786,-0.02407837,0.026138306,-0.023956299,0.020965576,0.022659302,-0.020095825,0.015716553,-0.01272583,0.0029773712,-0.012512207,-0.018539429,0.004547119,-0.015487671,-0.0027866364,0.01197052,-0.03262329,0.014289856,0.012664795,-0.011711121,-0.043151855,-0.0034618378,0.0010242462,-0.030334473,0.0029563904,0.009284973,0.007850647,-0.001789093,-0.03543091,0.04638672,-0.007774353,0.01663208,0.012428284,-0.015701294,0.0034255981,-0.014678955,-0.011077881,-0.038116455]'::public.vector,'title:Kotlin as a Safety Net: Type-Driven Reliability in the Finance Industry, description:What happens when a null pointer crash isn''t just a bug — it''s a compliance incident? Payment systems are one of the few places where the gap between "it compiles" and "it''s correct" has real financial and legal consequences. This is a story about using Kotlin''s type system as an architectural tool to close that gap.

Here''s what we''ll cover:
- Why Java wasn''t enough — the specific failure modes that triggered the migration: null crashes in transaction processing, broken rollback flows, and sensitive data leaking into logs at the worst possible moment
- Type-driven design in practice — how sealed hierarchies, value classes, and null-safety were used to make invalid payment states literally unrepresentable, and PCI-scoped data traceable through the type system — not through discipline
- Coroutines where it counts — structuring async money movement so that failure paths are explicit, not accidental
The ripple effect on the team — how stronger types changed code reviews, simplified testing, and turned audit prep from a scramble into a checklist
- Migrating without a rewrite — practical strategies for introducing Kotlin into a live system built on Java and C++
- Measuring what changed — concrete metrics: incident class reductions, compliance workflow improvements, and what actually moved the needle


You will learn a set of patterns you can apply immediately, even if payments are nowhere near your domain, because the core problem is universal: making correctness enforceable by the compiler, not by hope.','{"id": "07efc8877e3b4495a87c333aa8447585", "room": "Room 13a", "title": "Kotlin as a Safety Net: Type-Driven Reliability in the Finance Industry", "endsAt": 1779440400000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Server-side]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Yuri Geronimus]", "startsAt": 1779437700000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T11:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T10:15:00"}');
