.044525146,0.019607544,0.0023231506,-0.029541016,-0.027999878,-0.018371582,0.0049209595,-0.008529663,0.019561768,-0.012069702,-0.03378296,0.005050659,0.0061302185,0.045928955,0.033721924,0.0055770874,-0.031555176,0.018753052,0.0059013367,-0.028381348,-0.003255844,9.1433525e-05,0.006668091,0.031402588,0.009178162,0.001080513,-0.010406494,6.8604946e-05,0.027236938,-0.0051231384,-0.062438965,0.010978699,-0.0038986206,0.0050315857,0.014190674,-0.00063610077,-0.027023315,0.047821045,-0.0070877075,0.0043945312,0.03186035,-0.0011701584,0.03564453,-0.016235352,-0.030944824,-0.02468872,0.0039749146,-0.00920105,0.014381409,0.02859497,0.019943237,-0.01058197,0.029296875,-0.0022830963,0.010566711,-0.00415802,0.041900635,-0.0015420914,-0.012580872,0.002620697,0.013504028,0.013031006,-0.020446777,0.027328491,0.039031982,0.0038852692,-0.024276733,0.0206604,0.0011940002,0.012229919,-0.0057525635,-0.0028648376,-0.021347046,0.018218994,0.034576416,-0.016571045,0.013420105,-0.023635864,-0.013069153,-0.022064209,-0.040863037,0.009185791,-0.019622803,-0.004611969,0.0013198853,-0.02394104,0.032562256,-0.0061187744,0.00019109249,-0.009384155,-0.0098724365,-0.010543823,-0.03717041,0.01348114,-0.00010186434,-0.0137786865,-0.036712646,0.008087158,0.054718018,-0.026016235,0.030670166,0.026870728,-0.010955811,0.013923645,-0.008636475,0.00030326843,-0.038970947]'::public.vector,'title:How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love Value Semantics (in Kotlin), description:Kotlin developers often juggle references and mutable objects, both in server-side and mobile applications. In many cases, adopting value semantics makes code easier to read, reason about, and test. This session explains what value semantics actually is, why it matters, and how to apply it effectively in Kotlin without being afraid of a "complicated mental model" or "too many allocations."

The talk starts by clearly defining value semantics in contrast to reference semantics: identity vs equality, mutation vs copying, and how those ideas map to familiar Kotlin features such as data classes and their copy() function, up-and-coming value classes, and (im)mutable collections. It outlines the benefits of value-centric design: simpler reasoning about state, safer concurrency, fewer "who mutated this?" bugs, -- and then focuses on the real trade-offs: "more" apparent copying, potential allocation overhead, and changes in API and data model design.

Code examples and simple benchmarks demonstrate how value-semantics-oriented designs can still perform well on the JVM, and how to lean on features like value classes to make them practical. Attendees leave with a concrete mental model of value semantics in Kotlin, a realistic view of its pros and cons, and practical guidelines for where adopting it pays off and where it might not.','{"id": "eae500fa42264661a7d8a6fb468e62c1", "room": "Keynote, Room 14", "title": "How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love Value Semantics (in Kotlin)", "endsAt": 1779444000000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Language and best practices]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Marat Akhin]", "startsAt": 1779441300000, "phaseOfDay": "MORNING", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T12:00:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T11:15:00"}'),
