This was the directive given to a small group of Android engineers at Sony in 2021. The requirements were brutal: high-speed BLE connectivity for real-time sensor data, robust background execution, and a UI capable of keeping pace with hardware innovation. The goal? To ensure the software experience matched the world-class quality of our hardware.

Against conventional wisdom, we made a calculated bet on Kotlin Multiplatform (KMP), even though it was still in preview.

In this session, I will take you inside Sony’s 6-year journey from a risky experiment to a global success story: the Sony | Sound Connect app, now installed on millions of devices.

We’ll move far beyond simple code sharing and dive into the deep, hardware-driven challenges that pushed KMP to its limits. You will learn:

- The Hardware Challenge:
    How we balanced platform-specific BLE and background execution with shared KMP logic, and how we survived early threading and memory-model pain.

- The UI Evolution:
    Why we migrated from React Native to Compose Multiplatform while it was still in Beta, and how we integrated it with legacy native code to deliver a seamless Sony user experience.

- Navigating Stakeholder Fear:
    How we justified using “unstable” technology through risk analysis, fast iteration, and a lot of engineering scar tissue.

This is not a theoretical talk. It’s a behind-the-scenes story of trial, error, and eventual success, proving that Kotlin Multiplatform is ready for the most demanding, hardware-first environments.

As KMP and Compose Multiplatform reach full maturity in 2026, this talk offers the hindsight, patterns, and architectural lessons early adopters wish they had, and later adopters still need.','{"id": "34384484a1274873bbb9deae1871dbbb", "room": "Room 13b", "title": "Sony’s KMP Journey: Scaling BLE & Hardware with Kotlin Multiplatform", "endsAt": 1779450300000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Multiplatform, Other]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Sergio Carrilho]", "startsAt": 1779447600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T13:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T13:00:00"}'),
