In this session, I’ll show you how to use Kotlin LSP, command-line tools like emulators and adb, and Hot Reload to build and iterate on a KMP mobile app directly in Cursor. From there, I’ll take the next step and begin sharing Kotlin business logic with an existing JS/TS application—all within a single IDE, while maintaining a tight feedback loop. For the most adventurous, we might even try sharing some UI.

I will show also, how your web colleagues can use AI (Cursor in this case), to explore Kotlin code, without a need to spend months learning it.

Learning and using Kotlin is now easier outside of its traditional tooling, making it more attractive for teams to adopt KMP as a way to share business logic with web clients.','{"id": "cfa4d4f244a84c8281aff67c14740093", "room": "Room 5, Lightning talks", "title": "How your Web colleagues can start using Kotlin within their preferred environment?", "endsAt": 1779448500000, "category": "[Lightning talk, Introductory and overview, Multiplatform, AI, Other]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Denis Ambatenne]", "startsAt": 1779447600000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 15, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T13:15:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T13:00:00"}'),
