12451,-0.015777588,0.002084732,0.009651184,-0.0011138916,-0.0077209473,0.03881836,-0.006500244,-0.017532349,0.003873825,0.025436401,0.044067383,0.021636963,0.014160156,0.008895874,0.0099105835,-0.012168884,-0.029006958,-0.0067367554,0.004714966,0.02684021,0.024414062,0.015151978,-0.013977051,-0.020721436,-0.005859375,0.017974854,0.021438599,-0.0619812,-0.012336731,-0.021255493,0.020965576,0.011291504,-0.00541687,-0.017196655,0.03262329,0.0068206787,0.0001475811,0.012031555,0.00818634,0.011795044,0.016784668,-0.003873825,0.020614624,0.028427124,-0.008323669,0.005142212,0.0012807846,-0.0007739067,-0.008094788,0.025726318,-0.013694763,0.018234253,0.0045318604,0.031951904,-0.00831604,-0.0070991516,0.0008497238,-0.0036830902,0.0064582825,-0.05255127,0.028213501,-0.011291504,0.003522873,-0.026031494,-0.009605408,0.007003784,0.020385742,-0.02394104,0.01965332,-6.067753e-05,0.022827148,0.004512787,-0.011627197,0.010398865,-0.009338379,-0.00894165,-0.016143799,0.008148193,0.008460999,-0.026947021,-0.003019333,0.018814087,-0.013717651,0.0154418945,-0.0015420914,-0.0042686462,-0.02998352,0.0007376671,-0.005279541,-0.053497314,-0.008460999,-0.0009255409,0.0015487671,-0.02671814,-0.0028648376,0.049316406,-0.018508911,-0.0049934387,0.028717041,0.0036354065,0.006149292,-0.03353882,-0.02708435,-0.0357666]'::public.vector,'title:What Nobody Told Us About Shipping Kotlin to iOS, description:Three Android developers. No iOS experience. A two-month deadline to ship an internal app to both platforms. We went with Compose Multiplatform, and the UI layer worked well with 100% Compose. What nobody warned us about was everything underneath — the boundary between Swift and Kotlin.

After running the app in production for about a year, we''ve collected a set of problems and patterns worth sharing. This session covers three case studies from our production experience:
- Native State Observation: Using StateFlow as a bridge between iOS-native events and shared Compose UI — handling force updates, feature flags, and remote config with one pattern.
- Integrating Non-KMP SDKs: A delegate pattern for Firebase (which has no KMP SDK), and the binary isolation behavior we didn''t see coming.
- SPM Umbrella Export: The fix for binary isolation that keeps your Gradle modules separate while compiling into a single iOS binary.

This session includes a Swift-Kotlin interop pitfalls cheat sheet and the story of what happened when we brought this architecture to the JetBrains booth at KotlinConf 2025.','{"id": "5480296946a14495bdcbbba3a3dbc347", "room": "Room 13a", "title": "What Nobody Told Us About Shipping Kotlin to iOS", "endsAt": 1779453900000, "category": "[Regular session, Intermediate, Multiplatform, Android]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Suhyeon (Leah) Kim]", "startsAt": 1779451200000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 45, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T14:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T14:00:00"}'),
