This talk introduces the Backend Immune System, a Kotlin‑native resilience pattern built on three ecosystem strengths: rich errors, Ktor observability and local, deterministic agents that perform safe, verifiable remediations.

Inspired by biological immune systems, we explore how backend failures can be turned into actionable signals, how Ktor can act as an observability radar and how a lightweight local agent can respond with safe, idempotent micro‑remediations.

This is not self‑healing infrastructure and not a remote LLM. The agent operates strictly within the request boundary, performs only bounded, verifiable actions and escalates when necessary. It cannot patch Ktor, rewrite code, or fix systemic outages and that''s exactly why the pattern is safe and production‑credible.

A short demo shows three deterministic runs:
• Legacy mode: opaque exceptions and generic 500s
• Immune mode without verification: demonstrating why error disappearance is not success
• Immune mode with verification: showing correct, end‑to‑end recovery','{"id": "e9cc9f4de7284ec0ba9831adad987abb", "room": "Room 5, Lightning talks", "title": "The Backend Immune System - Rich Errors, Ktor Observability and Safe Local Remediation", "endsAt": 1779453900000, "category": "[Lightning talk, Introductory and overview, Server-side, Language and best practices]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Meike Felicia Hammer]", "startsAt": 1779453000000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 15, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T14:45:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T14:30:00"}'),
