In this short talk, I’ll walk through the journey of a language feature — from an early idea to a real part of Kotlin. You’ll see how language design decisions are made, where user research fits into this process, and how developer feedback helps shape features long before they are released.

This talk is not about technical internals, but about how language designers and researchers work together to make Kotlin evolve in a developer-centered way.','{"id": "edc62a29596c4e568f6a288c1a7b014f", "room": "Room 5, Lightning talks", "title": "From Idea to Feature: How Kotlin Language Design Uses User Research", "endsAt": 1779456600000, "category": "[Lightning talk, Introductory and overview, Language and best practices]", "dayIndex": 2, "speakers": "[Natalia Mishina]", "startsAt": 1779455700000, "phaseOfDay": "AFTERNOON", "conferenceDay": "2026-05-22", "durationMinutes": 15, "endsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T15:30:00", "startsAtLocalDateTime": "2026-05-22T15:15:00"}'),
