0014009476,-0.031921387,-0.039123535,-0.018569946,-0.0063591003,-0.016906738,-0.021774292,0.026306152,-0.0017385483,-0.006122589,0.009101868,0.008705139,0.039764404,-0.0007529259,-0.0057373047,-0.017715454,0.046722412,-0.0181427,-0.01108551,0.0046310425,0.03527832,0.010185242,0.037994385,0.009277344,0.015045166,-0.045837402,-0.002735138,-0.006462097,0.0034503937,-0.062316895,0.018585205,0.0061798096,0.0029144287,0.014152527,-0.031707764,-0.023361206,0.02633667,0.01802063,-0.009140015,-0.009971619,0.007205963,0.016708374,0.0035305023,-0.014083862,0.0020999908,0.010848999,-0.006866455,0.010665894,-0.010192871,0.008010864,-0.0036621094,0.013145447,0.018569946,0.019348145,0.006084442,0.0390625,-0.019760132,-0.031280518,-0.0011129379,-0.017181396,0.010887146,-0.020751953,0.033325195,0.00086307526,0.004096985,-0.009933472,0.019073486,0.008331299,0.016159058,-0.0064315796,0.0018644333,-0.026794434,0.012329102,0.012023926,-0.009666443,-0.02558899,0.011161804,-0.0067481995,-0.025543213,0.011154175,0.027374268,-0.0115737915,0.014480591,-0.0068626404,-0.017486572,0.005519867,-0.005542755,-0.010726929,0.0041160583,0.012741089,-0.0051956177,-0.019805908,0.014137268,-0.006038666,0.012268066,-0.026885986,-0.027450562,0.038085938,-0.024642944,0.026062012,0.027496338,-0.010505676,-0.0022392273,-0.014251709,-0.0010519028,-0.014915466]'::public.vector,'title:KotlinLLM: Leveraging AI for Runtime Logic Delegation in Kotlin, description:As software development evolves in the AI era, developers need adapt new technologies to improve efficiency and workflows. Kotlin, known for reducing boilerplate code and enabling clean, readable solutions, is an ideal foundation for integrating AI into modern development practices. Building on Kotlin’s core principles, we propose an idiomatic way to use large LLMs directly in Kotlin code: Runtime LLM Calls or shorter KotlinLLM.

Runtime LLM Calls allow LLMs to work with runtime context, going beyond static, predefined developer code. We introduce three key constructions - runLLM, asLLM, and catchLLM - each designed to address common development challenges:

- runLLM: Dynamically delegate logic to an LLM. This is especially useful for mocking, helping developers create stub implementations of interfaces and replace traditional TODOs in abstract method overriding.
- asLLM: Automate object casting (e.g., JSON, YAML) into Kotlin data classes using LLMs, simplifying serialization and deserialization workflows.
- catchLLM: Use LLM reasoning to gather context on runtime exceptions and produce detailed, meaningful error reports, making debugging faster and easier.

This initiative is being implemented as a MVP. While the technical details and naming may evolve, the goal remains the same: to demonstrate how AI can integrate seamlessly with Kotlin, empowering developers with smarter, more flexible tools.
