2298584,-0.01360321,0.0019721985,0.0001155138,-0.023513794,-0.034332275,0.0043640137,0.011779785,-0.012184143,0.029129028,-0.0016298294,-0.01007843,0.014945984,0.042388916,0.042755127,-0.0047340393,-0.010597229,0.03314209,-0.0025806427,-0.00459671,0.0039100647,-0.006893158,-0.005092621,0.012260437,0.026260376,-0.007194519,0.019256592,-0.037261963,-0.0075187683,0.032989502,-0.0059127808,-0.045837402,0.012435913,-0.0010919571,0.024520874,0.006034851,-0.009727478,-0.024276733,0.040985107,-0.027267456,-0.0095825195,0.009170532,0.02609253,0.054718018,-0.01626587,-0.0031032562,0.013450623,0.029449463,-0.013381958,0.024749756,0.002614975,0.02407837,-0.015396118,0.029190063,-0.019088745,-0.0037193298,0.021148682,0.0079956055,0.002254486,0.010574341,-0.022369385,-0.010032654,0.011413574,-0.039093018,0.011871338,0.00605011,0.029571533,0.0060424805,-0.0061569214,-0.0069122314,0.03302002,-0.03994751,0.030029297,-0.016693115,0.014434814,0.0029659271,-0.01084137,-0.018310547,0.0099487305,-0.015174866,-0.009414673,0.009010315,0.0027217865,-0.025115967,0.026611328,0.0017957687,-0.0017929077,0.028411865,-0.011116028,-0.026046753,-0.0012750626,0.018447876,-0.027328491,-0.046447754,-0.037200928,-0.02470398,0.01625061,-0.024108887,0.015312195,0.027572632,-0.015274048,0.005378723,0.04360962,-0.008071899,-0.008583069,-0.028640747,0.0072898865,-0.017959595]'::public.vector,'title:Developing Music Creation Apps for Web, Desktop and Android, description:Writing music creation apps for multiple platforms is challenging. We need cross platform, low latency audio. We also need a UI that can follow the musical events. And for multiplayer apps we need cross platform network communication.

Learn how we solved the audio problem using expected/actual classes and multiple host specific implementations, including WebAudio. We will examine the issues around using co-routines for real-time audio and how it relates to state flow. We will also discuss the conversion of an old general purpose modular synthesis library from Java to Kotlin Multiplatform and how we modernized the API.

The UI required some novel techniques including custom drawing and adaptive layout. We will also show how to support drag gestures using either a mouse or a finger.

For networking we used ktor and also developed a server based relay to transfer web-socket traffic to our existing TCP/IP based collaboration server.

We will demonstrate the use of these techniques in two music creation apps. One is a multiplayer online drum machine. The other is a practice app that shows fingering for guitar and keyboard as your custom composition is played.

If you are building real-time audio applications then this session will provide the architectural insights and production-ready techniques that can help you succeed.
